<speak>
From <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news, this is your daily briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">16022026</say-as>.
<break time="1s"/>
In politics.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">Bangladesh</emphasis> elected the Nationalist Party in a landslide, securing two hundred nine of two hundred ninety-seven seats. Tarique Rahman, who returned from seventeen years of exile, will be sworn in as prime minister by February eighteenth in the first vote since student uprisings toppled the previous government.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">Iran</emphasis> and the United States resume nuclear talks in Geneva on Tuesday, with Tehran offering to reduce enrichment from sixty percent to twenty percent. Israeli Prime Minister <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="nɛtaˈnjahu">Netanyahu</phoneme> demands full dismantlement of Iran's nuclear program, creating tensions with Washington over diplomatic strategy.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">Hungary</emphasis> enters its strongest electoral contest in sixteen years as opposition leader <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈmaɡjaʁ">Magyar</phoneme> pledges to unlock frozen European Union funds. Independent polls show his <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈtisɒ">Tisza</phoneme> party leading Prime Minister <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈorbaːn">Orban</phoneme> by double digits ahead of the April twelfth parliamentary election.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">Australia</emphasis> committed three point nine billion Australian dollars to a submarine facility under the AUKUS alliance with Britain and America. The program, which could reach two hundred thirty-five billion dollars over thirty years, aims to counter China's expanding Indo-Pacific naval presence.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">China</emphasis> granted thirty-day visa-free entry to British and Canadian nationals, adding them to seventy-seven countries already enjoying access. The United States remains excluded, isolating Washington from its Five Eyes intelligence partners as Canada dropped tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles before the announcement.
<break time="900ms"/>
In conflict and security.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">African Union</emphasis> leaders demanded an immediate ceasefire in Sudan, where eleven million people have fled civil war approaching its third year. United Nations Secretary-General <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="anˈtɔnju ɡuˈtɛrɨʃ">Guterres</phoneme> backed the call, but Sudan remains suspended from the bloc with no peace process underway.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">Israel</emphasis> killed ten Palestinians in overnight strikes ahead of Thursday's Board of Peace summit in Washington. Deaths since October's ceasefire now total six hundred one, while the reconstruction board convenes without Palestinian representation despite five billion dollars pledged by twenty-five countries.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">United States forces</emphasis> withdrew from Syria's al-Shaddadi base, reducing troop levels from fifteen hundred to nine hundred. Kurdish communities that relied on American security guarantees now face a Syrian government reasserting control over territory lost during the civil war.
<break time="900ms"/>
On the economy.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">India</emphasis> agreed to stop buying Russian crude oil, reversing its position as Moscow's largest customer since Western sanctions began. Secretary of State <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈmɑːrkoʊ ˈruːbioʊ">Rubio</phoneme> announced the commitment in Munich, though neither government set a public timeline for replacing cheap barrels that account for over a third of Indian imports.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">The European Union</emphasis> banned companies from destroying unsold clothing and footwear starting July this year. The regulation targets fashion's overproduction cycle, which generates five point six million tons of carbon dioxide annually, requiring firms to resell, donate, or remanufacture surplus stock.
<break time="600ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">Zambia</emphasis> attracted twelve billion dollars to its copper sector since twenty twenty-two, raising output eight percent last year. Electric vehicle and data centre demand drives the boom, positioning the metal as a geopolitical prize between Chinese-owned smelters and American critical-minerals initiatives.
<break time="900ms"/>
In technology.<break time="400ms"/>
<emphasis level="moderate">India</emphasis> secured one hundred seventy-five billion dollars across seven hundred sixty projects, led by Google's fifteen-billion-dollar artificial intelligence hub in <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ʋiʃaːkʰʌpʌʈnʌm">Visakhapatnam</phoneme>. The government approved six nuclear plants to power the six-gigawatt data centre city, challenging American and Chinese dominance in AI computing infrastructure.
<break time="1s"/>
That's your briefing from <phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="zʊhd">zuhd</phoneme> news.
</speak>